From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: x <x@x>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] 0152: blueline files pushed into the root at boot (bluepush=)

Makes a userland change as quick to test as a kernel change: no image
round trip, no sudo, no fastboot flash.

tools/iosfs-push.sh (LineageOS recovery, adb) writes a payload of files
into the 256 MiB of userdata right below the 0146 screenshot area (beyond
the end of the 16 GiB HFS+ root). With bluepush=1, right after the root is
mounted and before launchd runs, bsd/dev/blueline_payload.c reads it
through the root device's strategy routine (read only), checks magic,
size and CRC-32 of the whole payload, and applies each entry (file,
directory, remove) through the HFS+ driver: mkdir -p, unlink, vnode_open +
vn_rdwr, owner and mode, fsync. The driver handles catalog, extents and
journal. The files are on the root volume, so ad-hoc signed binaries are
platform binaries there (sweet_amfi.c), and they persist. bluepush=2 only
lists the entries. The area is used only when the HFS+ volume header says
the volume ends before it.

The test runner (tools/bluerun, boot-arg bluerun=) is user space and needs
nothing more from the kernel: it is pushed like any other file, started
by a launchd job, and writes its child's output to /dev/console, which
lands in the persistent log (0047).

